### Credentials — never commit these ###
#
# The distinction that matters is between an identifier and a credential.
#
# google-services.json is NOT here. It holds a project id, an app id and an
# Android API key, all of which are extractable from any distributed APK in
# seconds. They identify the project; they authorise nothing. What protects the
# data is database.rules.json. Firebase's own guidance is to commit it, and
# without it neither continuous integration nor a teammate's checkout can build
# the live flavour.
#
# The three below are genuine credentials. A service-account key grants full
# administrative access and bypasses every security rule; a keystore lets its
# holder sign an application as though they were its author.
serviceAccountKey.json
worker/serviceAccountKey.json
*.keystore
*.jks
keystore.properties

### Machine-local ###
local.properties
worker/.env
simulator/firebase-config.js

### Android / Gradle ###
*.iml
.gradle/
.idea/
.DS_Store
build/
captures/
.externalNativeBuild
.cxx
release/
*.apk
*.aab
*.ap_
*.dex

# Exception: the submitted builds in apk/ are a deliverable and are committed
# deliberately. Build output elsewhere remains excluded by the rule above.
!apk/*.apk

### Node ###
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
worker/dist/
worker/coverage/

